{"id":"security-linux-kernel-security-constraints","corpus":"k8s","title":"Linux kernel security constraints for Pods and containers","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/linux-kernel-security-constraints/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Linux kernel security constraints for Pods and containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"Overview of Linux kernel security modules and constraints that you can use to harden your Pods and containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page describes some of the security features that are built into the Linux kernel that you can use in your Kubernetes workloads. To learn how to apply these features to your Pods and containers, refer to Configure a SecurityContext for a Pod or Container. You should already be familiar with Linux and with the basics of Kubernetes workloads."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Run workloads without root privileges"},{"kind":"p","text":"When you deploy a workload in Kubernetes, use the Pod specification to restrict that workload from running as the root user on the node. You can use the Pod securityContext to define the specific Linux user and group for the processes in the Pod, and explicitly restrict containers from running as root users. Setting these values in the Pod manifest takes precedence over similar values in the container image, which is especially useful if you're running images that you don't own."},{"kind":"p","text":"Ensure that the user or group that you assign to the workload has the permissions required for the application to function correctly. Changing the user or group to one that doesn't have the correct permissions could lead to file access issues or failed operations."},{"kind":"p","text":"Configuring the kernel security features on this page provides fine-grained control over the actions that processes in your cluster can take, but managing these configurations can be challenging at scale. Running containers as non-root, or in user namespaces if you need root privileges, helps to reduce the chance that you'll need to enforce your configured kernel security capabilities."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Security features in the Linux kernel"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes lets you configure and use Linux kernel features to improve isolation and harden your containerized workloads. Common features include the following:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Secure computing mode (seccomp): Filter which system calls a process can make"},{"kind":"li","text":"AppArmor: Restrict the access privileges of individual programs"},{"kind":"li","text":"Security Enhanced Linux (SELinux): Assign security labels to objects for more manageable security policy enforcement"},{"kind":"p","text":"To configure settings for one of these features, the operating system that you choose for your nodes must enable the feature in the kernel. For example, Ubuntu 7.10 and later enable AppArmor by default. To learn whether your OS enables a specific feature, consult the OS documentation."},{"kind":"p","text":"You use the securityContext field in your Pod specification to define the constraints that apply to those processes. The securityContext field also supports other security settings, such as specific Linux capabilities or file access permissions using UIDs and GIDs. To learn more, refer to Configure a SecurityContext for a Pod or Container."},{"kind":"h3","text":"seccomp"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some of your workloads might need privileges to perform specific actions as the root user on your node's host machine. Linux uses capabilities to divide the available privileges into categories, so that processes can get the privileges required to perform specific actions without being granted all privileges. Each capability has a set of system calls (syscalls) that a process can make. seccomp lets you restrict these individual syscalls. It can be used to sandbox the privileges of a process, restricting the calls it is able to make from userspace into the kernel."},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, you use a container runtime on each node to run your containers. Example runtimes include CRI-O, Docker, or containerd. Each runtime allows only a subset of Linux capabilities by default. You can further limit the allowed syscalls individually by using a seccomp profile. Container runtimes usually include a default seccomp profile. Kubernetes lets you automatically apply seccomp profiles loaded onto a node to your Pods and containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes also has the allowPrivilegeEscalation setting for Pods and containers. When set to false, this prevents processes from gaining new capabilities and restricts unprivileged users from changing the applied seccomp profile to a more permissive profile."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn how to implement seccomp in Kubernetes, refer to Restrict a Container's Syscalls with seccomp or the Seccomp node reference"},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn more about seccomp, see Seccomp BPF in the Linux kernel documentation."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Considerations for seccomp"},{"kind":"p","text":"seccomp is a low-level security configuration that you should only configure yourself if you require fine-grained control over Linux syscalls. Using seccomp, especially at scale, has the following risks:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Configurations might break during application updates"}],"lemmas":{"linuxes":["linux","linuxes"],"kernels":["kernel","kernels"],"securities":["security","securities"],"constraints":["constraint","constraints"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"containers":["container","containers"],"overviews":["overview","overviews"],"modules":["module","modules"],"hardens":["hardens","harden"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"describes":["describes","describe"],"described":["described","describe"],"describing":["describing","describe"],"features":["feature","features"],"featuring":["featurin